141
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:11,380
Today we are honored to have Professor Barnett, who is an expert in dolphin study,

142
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:16,200
and he will introduce to us the unique characteristics and living conditions of

143
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:18,940
dolphins. Professor Barnett, please.

144
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:22,160
Thank you for your introduction, Lisa.

145
00:14:22,780 --> 00:14:27,320
Well, one of the most intelligent animals on the planet is the dolphin.

146
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:32,620
These unique animals have been regarded highly by humans for many centuries.

147
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:38,340
In ancient Greece, if sailors saw dolphins swim by their ships when they were

148
00:14:38,340 --> 00:14:42,520
starting out on a voyage, they believed they would have good luck.

149
00:14:43,220 --> 00:14:47,680
One unique characteristic that makes them different from other sea animals is the

150
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:49,160
way that they communicate.

151
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:55,320
use sound, touch, and taste in order to learn about their world.

152
00:14:55,640 --> 00:14:59,320
However, unlike dogs, dolphins cannot smell.

153
00:14:59,320 --> 00:15:04,460
They use a whistle or sound waves to see and feel their way around their

154
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:09,900
environment. This makes them different from many other sea animals and also helps

155
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:11,740
them communicate with people.

156
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:17,200
Another unique thing about dolphins is that the bones in their fins are similar to

157
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:19,460
those in human arms and hands.

158
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:24,760
However, the connective tissue that surrounds the bones forms a flipper instead of a

159
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,660
hand with individual fingers.

160
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:32,080
Even though dolphins live in the oceans, they do not drink the salt water around

161
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:37,260
them. Their bodies absorb water from the wide variety of creatures they eat.

162
00:15:38,620 --> 00:15:43,740
Loving a dolphin is an easy thing to do, but unlike a dog or a cat, the dolphin

163
00:15:43,740 --> 00:15:45,960
cannot come home with you from the ocean.

164
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:50,100
That is why many people like collecting dolphin souvenirs.

165
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:55,240
One can find many such things in almost every shop around the world.

166
00:15:55,240 --> 00:16:00,240
Even the ancient people painted the images of dolphins on their vases.

167
00:16:01,300 --> 00:16:07,240
However, dolphins are sometimes hunted in places like Japan in an activity known as

168
00:16:07,240 --> 00:16:09,200
dolphin drive hunting.

169
00:16:09,720 --> 00:16:14,620
Besides drive hunting, they also face threats from habitat loss and marine

170
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:20,300
pollution. Dolphins have been depicted in various cultures worldwide.

171
00:16:21,300 --> 00:16:24,200
Dolphins occasionally feature in literature and film.

172
00:16:24,200 --> 00:16:29,350
Dolphins are sometimes kept in captivity and trained to perform tricks.

173
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:35,520
But breeding success has been poor, and the animals often die within a few months of

174
00:16:35,520 --> 00:16:41,060
capture. The most common dolphins kept are the killer whales and the bottlenose

